[interviewer · INTRO] Your client is Brew & Bean, a specialty coffee chain with 200 locations across the US. Over the past two years, their net profit margin has declined from 24% to 6%, even though revenues have grown 15% over the same period. The CEO has hired us to identify the root cause of this profit decline and recommend how to reverse it. How would you approach this problem?

Take a minute if you need it, then tell me how you'd structure your approach.

[candidate silent 25s]

[candidate · 1:55] Okay, so the headline is margin collapsing from 24% to 6% — that's an 18-point drop, roughly three quarters of the margin gone — while revenue grew 15%. So this isn't a demand problem, it's a cost problem until proven otherwise. I'd estimate costs are growing much faster than the top line.

Quick sanity check with round numbers: if revenue was 100 before, profit was 24, costs 76. Now revenue is 115, profit is about 7 — call it 6% of 115, so ~6.9 — so costs are roughly 108. That's 76 to 108, about 42% cost growth against 15% revenue growth. Estimating, but the gap is the whole story.

So I'd structure it as: one, revenue quality — is the 15% from more stores, more traffic, or price? Two, cost structure — COGS like beans and milk, labor, rent, and corporate overhead, and which line moved. Three, mix effects from new stores dragging.

Can I start with the cost breakdown by line, then and now?
