[interviewer · EXHIBIT · 8:10] Check the 37.5% against how you applied it: was that figure a unit-cost increase or a total spend increase? Separately, I don't have the bean contract or hedging detail. Is there a bean price figure you'd want me to give you to test your residual?

[candidate · 9:40] Good challenge. I assumed 37.5% was a unit-cost increase, but if it's total spend it already bakes in the ~15% volume growth — so the true unit-cost rise would be 37.5 over 1.15, about 19%, roughly half what I used. That materially changes the split. Which is it?

If it's total spend: old other-inputs index 31.5, up 37.5% = ~43. Today's COGS ~67, so beans ≈ 67 − 43 = 24, versus a volume-adjusted ~12 — that's beans doubling in unit cost. So the interpretation flips who the main driver is: under "unit cost" it's balanced; under "total spend" beans dominate.

The figure I'd want to test the residual is the actual bean price per pound then versus now — a wholesale index would do. If beans roughly doubled, it confirms the total-spend reading and points straight at coffee procurement as the single biggest lever.
